  data:  # a99e
    kind: work_item  # a99e
    title: "structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol: add 'When NOT to file a new tracker item' section"  # a99e
    status: todo_soft  # a99e
    priority: 3  # a99e
    parent:  # a99e
    tags:  # a99e
    - agent_governance  # a99e
    - developer_experience  # a99e
    isbefore: []  # a99e
    duplicate_of: []  # a99e
    not_duplicate_of: []  # a99e
    pr_ref:  # a99e
    description: "Add a \"When NOT to create a new item\" section to `.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol.md` to prevent cargo-culted tracker-item creation.\n\n**Motivating incident (session_retrospective_04262026_1911.md Finding 6 / timeline 06:49):** After spotting an auto-pr Scenario B issue mid-conversation, the agent reflexively filed a 4th tracker item interpreting `structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol` as \"always file tracker items for newly-discovered concerns.\" The user pushed back (\"you didn't need to but you thought you did\") and the agent rolled the item back. Damage was trivial in the moment (one extra item, undone) but the pattern is the concern: the playbook successfully primes \"file tracker items\" without successfully priming \"but only when filing advances work, not when conversation is sufficient.\"\n\n**Proposed addition** (verbatim sketch — exact wording to be polished in the PR):\n\n```\n### When NOT to file a new tracker item\n\nThe Scope Expansion Commitment Protocol pushes hard toward filing items for\nanalogous issues across languages/constructs/pipeline stages. That bias is\ncorrect *as a default*, but it has three failure modes you should recognize\nand route around.\n\n1. **Existing-coverage check.** Before filing, run `scripts/tracker list\n   --tag <plausible-tag>` or `tracker list --kind <invariant|work_item>` and\n   spot-check titles. If an existing item already covers the surface, prefer\n   `tracker discuss <ID>` with a regression note rather than a new item. New\n   items fragment the discussion thread the human is reading.\n\n2. **Conversation-in-progress check.** If the human is actively engaged in\n   the conversation that surfaced the concern, ask before filing. The\n   default response shape \"I'll file a tracker item for that\" can be exactly\n   wrong when the human's intent was \"let's discuss this and decide\n   together what to do.\" Cheap fix: surface the proposed item title +\n   one-line rationale and ask \"file this as <ID-class>?\" before invoking\n   `tracker add`.\n\n3. **Property-of-existing-invariant check.** A new failure mode that is\n   structurally a *property* of an already-tracked invariant should be\n   filed as a `tracker discuss` regression note on the parent invariant,\n   not as a new INV. The aggregate-of-properties view of an invariant is\n   what makes it useful for cross-session pattern recognition; splitting\n   the regression into a sibling item destroys that aggregation.\n\nThe default remains \"when in doubt, use `todo_hard`.\" This addition only\nnarrows the default for three specific shapes that empirically produce\ncargo-cult filings.\n```\n\n**Cross-reference paragraph to add to the existing Commitment Protocol section:** \"See also §When NOT to file a new tracker item below — the default to-file bias has three exceptions worth recognizing.\"\n\n**Why this is non-governance:** `.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/` is not in the AGENTS.md governance allowlist. Only `.agent/hooks/` and a handful of named scripts are governance. The playbook can be edited and merged via standard auto-pr without the needs_human_review gate. The corresponding hook summary in `.agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py` PLAYBOOKS list should be expanded to include the \"when NOT to file\" framing — that file IS governance, so the hook-summary update needs human review on its own.\n\n**Implementation steps:**\n1. Edit the playbook file directly with the new section + cross-reference paragraph.\n2. Update the playbook's hook summary in `on_transcript_change.py:PLAYBOOKS` to mention the negative cases (`needs_human_review` on this step alone).\n3. No AGENTS.md edit required — the existing essentialization already references the playbook by path; readers who need the \"when not to\" branch will load the playbook on demand.\n\n**Tests:** the playbook change is documentation; coverage is via the hook test that asserts the summary text contains both positive and negative trigger phrases. Add a test if not present.\n\n**Cross-references:**\n- session_retrospective_04262026_1911.md Finding 6 (motivating incident).\n- `.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol.md` (file being edited).\n- `.agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py` (hook summary, governance, separate review)."  # a99e
    fields: {}  # a99e
